⭐ AMI (Amazon Machine Image)
AMI (Amazon Machine Image) is a pre-configured template used to launch an EC2 instance. 
It contains the operating system, application software, and configuration required to create a virtual server.
it includes:
-operating system(ex. Linux, window)
-Application server(eg:apachi, nginx)
-pre-installed and configuration


>> What are the types of AMIs?
1. Public AMIs: Available to all AWS users. Useful for basic use cases like popular operating systems (e.g., Ubuntu, CentOS).
2. Private AMIs: Created by a user and only available within that account or shared with specific accounts.
3. Paid AMIs/Marketplace AMIs: 
Provided by third parties through AWS Marketplace, offering software like databases, web servers, or pre-configured environments.

>> Why do we use an AMI?
We use an AMI to launch EC2 instances quickly without installing the operating system and software every time.

>> What does an AMI contain?
An AMI contains:
Operating System (Linux/Windows)
Application software
Configuration settings
EBS snapshot (storage)

>> Suppose you have installed Java, Tomcat, and your application on an EC2 instance. You need to create 10 identical servers. What will you do?

I will create an AMI from the configured EC2 instance. Then I can launch multiple EC2 instances from that AMI. Each new instance will have the same OS, software, and configuration.

>> Can we create our own AMI?
Yes. After configuring an EC2 instance with the required software and settings, we can create a custom AMI and reuse it whenever needed.


>> What is the difference between an AMI and an EC2 instance?
AMI is a template.
EC2 is a running virtual server created from an AMI.
Example:
AMI = Blueprint of a house 🏠
EC2 = Actual house built from the blueprint.

>> Can one AMI be used to launch multiple EC2 instances?
Yes. A single AMI can be used to launch any number of EC2 instances.

>> If you update your application on one EC2 instance, will the AMI update automatically?

No. An AMI does not update automatically. If changes are made to an EC2 instance, we must create a new AMI to include those changes.

Interviewer: Your company needs 50 web servers with the same configuration. How will you deploy them quickly?

Configure one EC2 instance with the required OS, Java, Tomcat, and application.
Create a custom AMI from that instance.
Launch 50 EC2 instances using the custom AMI.
Optionally, use an Auto Scaling Group to manage the instances automatically.

