⭐ Elastic Beanstalk
Elastic Beanstalk is a Platform as a Service (PaaS) offered by Amazon Web Services. It allows developers to deploy and manage applications without worrying about the underlying infrastructure. AWS automatically handles provisioning, load balancing, auto scaling, and monitoring.

>> Why do we use Elastic Beanstalk?
We use Elastic Beanstalk because it:
Makes application deployment easy.
Automatically manages infrastructure.
Supports Auto Scaling and Load Balancing.
Reduces server management effort.

>> Which programming languages does Elastic Beanstalk support?
PHP
.NET
Ruby
Go
Docker applications

>> Suppose you developed a Spring Boot application. How will you deploy it using Elastic Beanstalk?

-Build the Spring Boot JAR file.
-Open the AWS Management Console.
-Create a new Elastic Beanstalk application.
-Select the Java platform.
-Upload the JAR file.
-AWS automatically creates the EC2 instance, Load Balancer, Security Groups, and Auto Scaling Group.
-Access the application using the generated URL.

>> Does Elastic Beanstalk create EC2 instances?
Yes. Elastic Beanstalk automatically creates and manages EC2 instances, Load Balancers, Auto Scaling Groups, and other required AWS resources.

>> Can we access the EC2 instance created by Elastic Beanstalk?
Yes. Since Elastic Beanstalk uses EC2 in the background, we can SSH into the EC2 instance if needed.

>> Can we scale applications in Elastic Beanstalk?
Yes. Elastic Beanstalk supports Auto Scaling. It can automatically add or remove EC2 instances based on traffic or CPU usage.

>> What AWS services does Elastic Beanstalk use internally?
Elastic Beanstalk commonly uses:
EC2
Auto Scaling
Elastic Load Balancer (ELB)
CloudWatch

>> Your company developed a Java web application. The developers don't want to manage servers. What AWS service would you recommend?

I would recommend Elastic Beanstalk. Developers only need to upload the application. AWS automatically provisions the infrastructure, deploys the application, configures load balancing and auto scaling, and monitors the environment. This lets the team focus on writing code instead of managing servers.


# ☁️ AWS Lambda
AWS Lambda is a serverless computing service provided by Amazon Web Services. 
It allows us to run code without provisioning or managing servers. 
We simply upload our code, and Lambda executes it when triggered by an event.

>> Why do we use AWS Lambda?
We use AWS Lambda because
No server management is required.
We pay only for the execution time.
It scales automatically.
It is event-driven.

>> What can trigger a Lambda function?
A Lambda function can be triggered by:
S3 (file upload)
API Gateway (HTTP request)
DynamoDB
CloudWatch Events/EventBridge

>> Suppose a user uploads an image to an S3 bucket. How will Lambda help?
I will configure an S3 event trigger. Whenever a user uploads an image:

-S3 triggers the Lambda function.
-Lambda processes the image (for example, resizes or -compresses it).
Lambda stores the processed image back in S3.

This process is automatic and requires no server management.

>> Event-driven execution
>> when to use lemda

>> Do we need to manage servers in Lambda?
No. AWS manages the servers, scaling, and infrastructure automatically. We only write and deploy the code.

>> How is Lambda pricing calculated?
Lambda charges based on:
- Number of requests
- Execution duration (milliseconds)
- Memory allocated

>> What is the maximum execution time of a Lambda function?
A Lambda function can run for a maximum of 15 minutes per invocation.

>> Your company receives thousands of invoices every day. Whenever a PDF is uploaded to S3, customer details should be extracted automatically. Which AWS service would you use?
I would use AWS Lambda.

Customer uploads the PDF to S3.
S3 triggers the Lambda function.
Lambda extracts the required information (or calls another service for extraction).
Lambda stores the processed data in a database such as DynamoDB or RDS.

>> Your website has a Contact Us form. When a user submits it, an email should be sent automatically. How would you implement this?

Candidate:
The website sends the request to API Gateway.
API Gateway invokes a Lambda function.
Lambda validates the data and sends the email (for example, using Amazon SES).
Lambda returns a success response to the website.



⭐ AWS ELB service
ELB stands for "Elastic Load Balancing".
It is an AWS service that automatically distributes incoming traffic across multiple EC2 instances or other targets.

Simple Example:
Suppose my website gets 10,000 users at the same time. ELB distributes the users across different servers so that one server does not become overloaded.

