⭐ AWS ASG service
ASG stands for "Auto Scaling Group".
It is an AWS feature that automatically increases or decreases the number of EC2 instances based on traffic or demand.

Simple Example:
If website traffic increases 📈, ASG automatically adds EC2 instances. If traffic decreases 📉, ASG automatically removes extra EC2 instances.

ELB + ASG Together
ELB = Distributes traffic 🚦
ASG = Adds or removes servers
Interview line:
“ELB distributes incoming traffic, while ASG automatically adjusts the number of EC2 instances based on demand.”



⭐ Vertical Scalability (Scaling Up)
Vertical Scalability means adding more power like CPU and RAM to the existing server.
Example: t2.micro → m5.large

Simple Hindi: Ek hi server ko zyada powerful banana.

⭐ Horizontal Scalability (Scaling Out)
Horizontal Scalability means adding more servers or EC2 instances to distribute the load.
Example: 1 EC2 Instance → 3 EC2 Instances

Simple Hindi: Ek server ko powerful banane ke bajay aur servers add karna.

Example: CPU/RAM increase = Vertical | EC2 instances increase = Horizontal


⭐ High Availability (HA)
High Availability means keeping your application or service running with minimum downtime, so users can access it anytime.
Example: Running resources in multiple Availability Zones (AZs).

Simple Hindi: Application ko hamesha available rakhna aur downtime ko minimum karna.

⭐ Elasticity
Elasticity means automatically increasing or decreasing resources based on demand or traffic.
Example: ASG (Auto Scaling Group) in AWS.

Simple Hindi: Demand ke according resources ko automatically add ya remove karna.

Simple Example: Website par traffic increase 📈 → more EC2 instances add. Traffic decrease 📉 → extra EC2 instances remove.